research/audit/wave6-topology.provenance.jsonl-20-{"id":"ex-a-finite-hausdorff-space-is-discrete","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://www.dpmms.cam.ac.uk/~or257/teaching/notes/TopSp.pdf"],"rationale":"The Cambridge notes explicitly state that the cofinite topology on a finite set is discrete and, equivalently through closed singletons, every finite Hausdorff space is discrete.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave6-topology.provenance.jsonl-21-{"id":"ex-separating-a-point-from-a-finite-set-in-a-hausdorff-space","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://pi.math.cornell.edu/~hatcher/Top/TopNotes.pdf"],"rationale":"Hatcher proves the stronger point-versus-compact and compact-versus-compact separation statements in a Hausdorff space. The item specializes the compact set to a finite set and records the finite-choice bookkeeping.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave6-topology.provenance.jsonl-22-{"id":"def-homotopy-relative-and-path-homotopy","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"not-applicable","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://pi.math.cornell.edu/~hatcher/AT/AT.pdf"],"rationale":"Hatcher defines homotopy, homotopy relative to a subspace, and path homotopy relative to endpoints with the same interval, endpoint, and fixed-subspace conventions.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave6-topology.provenance.jsonl-23-{"id":"lem-homotopy-reflexive-and-symmetric","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.math.unipd.it/~maraston/Topologia2/Topo2_1011_homotopy.pdf"],"rationale":"The notes state that homotopy is reflexive and symmetric as part of its equivalence-relation proof. The item retains a fixed subspace and supplies the explicit time reversal.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave6-topology.provenance.jsonl-24-{"id":"lem-homotopy-transitivity-by-reparametrisation","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.math.unipd.it/~maraston/Topologia2/Topo2_1011_homotopy.pdf"],"rationale":"The source proves transitivity by the finite closed-set gluing lemma; the item writes the reparametrized two-piece homotopy and preserves a fixed subspace.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave6-topology.provenance.jsonl-25-{"id":"cor-homotopy-relative-and-path-homotopy-are-equivalence-relations","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.math.unipd.it/~maraston/Topologia2/Topo2_1011_homotopy.pdf","https://pi.math.cornell.edu/~hatcher/AT/AT.pdf"],"rationale":"The sources state homotopy is an equivalence relation; the item records the same result for the fixed-restriction and fixed-endpoint subcollections.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave6-topology.provenance.jsonl:26:{"id":"thm-composition-respects-homotopy","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://math.ucr.edu/~jbergner/SPWM09.pdf"],"rationale":"The source states that composition preserves homotopy. The item separates precomposition and postcomposition and tracks the relative subspace.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave6-topology.provenance.jsonl-27-{"id":"def-nullhomotopic-map-and-contractible-space","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"not-applicable","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://pi.math.cornell.edu/~hatcher/AT/AT.pdf"],"rationale":"Hatcher defines nullhomotopic maps and contractibility by homotopy type of a point, and Exercise 10 gives the equivalent every-map-out formulation adopted here. The nonempty hypothesis makes the equivalence literal.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave6-topology.provenance.jsonl-28-{"id":"cor-contractible-iff-identity-nullhomotopic","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://pi.math.cornell.edu/~hatcher/AT/AT.pdf"],"rationale":"Hatcher states exactly that a space is contractible precisely when its identity is homotopic to a constant map.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave6-topology.provenance.jsonl-29-{"id":"def-homotopy-equivalence","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"not-applicable","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://pi.math.cornell.edu/~hatcher/AT/AT.pdf"],"rationale":"Hatcher gives the same two maps and both composite homotopies as the definition of homotopy equivalence.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave6-topology.provenance.jsonl-30-{"id":"thm-homotopy-equivalence-is-an-equivalence-relation","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://pi.math.cornell.edu/~hatcher/AT/AT.pdf"],"rationale":"Hatcher explicitly states that homotopy equivalence is an equivalence relation. The item supplies the composition calculation omitted there as an easy exercise.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave6-topology.provenance.jsonl-31-{"id":"cor-a-map-homotopic-to-a-homotopy-equivalence-is-a-homotopy-equivalence","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://pi.math.cornell.edu/~hatcher/AT/AT.pdf"],"rationale":"Hatcher explicitly uses the fact that a map homotopic to a homotopy equivalence is a homotopy equivalence, with the same unrestricted spaces and maps.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave6-topology.provenance.jsonl-32-{"id":"def-retraction-and-deformation-retract","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"not-applicable","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://pi.math.cornell.edu/~hatcher/AT/AT.pdf"],"rationale":"Hatcher defines a retraction and a deformation retraction with the subspace fixed at all times, exactly matching this library's convention.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave6-topology.provenance.jsonl-33-{"id":"thm-a-deformation-retract-is-a-homotopy-equivalence","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://pi.math.cornell.edu/~hatcher/AT/AT.pdf"],"rationale":"Hatcher writes the inclusion and retraction identities and homotopy that make a deformation retract a homotopy equivalence.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave6-topology.provenance.jsonl-34-{"id":"cor-a-retract-of-a-contractible-space-is-contractible","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://pi.math.cornell.edu/~hatcher/AT/AT.pdf"],"rationale":"Hatcher Chapter 0, Exercise 9, is exactly the assertion that a retract of a contractible space is contractible.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave6-topology.provenance.jsonl-35-{"id":"cor-homotopy-equivalence-bijection-on-path-components","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://pi.math.cornell.edu/~hatcher/AT/AT.pdf"],"rationale":"Hatcher Chapter 0, Exercise 12, states exactly that a homotopy equivalence induces a bijection on path components.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave6-topology.provenance.jsonl:36:{"id":"lem-straight-line-homotopies-are-continuous","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://math.ucr.edu/~jbergner/SPWM09.pdf"],"rationale":"The UC Riverside notes use the same straight-line formula for maps into a convex Euclidean subset. This item separately discharges continuity into the subspace codomain and therefore is a detailed semantic expansion.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave6-topology.provenance.jsonl:37:{"id":"thm-two-maps-into-a-convex-set-are-homotopic","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://math.ucr.edu/~jbergner/SPWM09.pdf"],"rationale":"The source states that any two continuous maps into a convex Euclidean set are homotopic by the straight-line homotopy.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave6-topology.provenance.jsonl:38:{"id":"cor-convex-subsets-of-rn-are-contractible","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://math.ucr.edu/~jbergner/SPWM09.pdf"],"rationale":"The source states and proves that every convex subset of Euclidean space is contractible by the same formula.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave6-topology.provenance.jsonl-39-{"id":"cor-contractible-spaces-are-path-connected","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://www.math.unipd.it/~maraston/Topologia2/Topo2_1011_homotopy.pdf"],"rationale":"The source states that a contractible space is path-connected and proves it by restricting the contraction to each point times the interval.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave6-topology.provenance.jsonl-40-{"id":"thm-punctured-rn-deformation-retracts-onto-the-sphere","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://www.math.stonybrook.edu/~cschnell/pdf/notes/mat530.pdf"],"rationale":"The Stony Brook notes give the same sphere, punctured Euclidean space, radial retraction, and straight-line deformation formula.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave6-topology.provenance.jsonl-41-{"id":"cor-punctured-rn-homotopy-equivalent-to-the-sphere","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://www.math.stonybrook.edu/~cschnell/pdf/notes/mat530.pdf"],"rationale":"The Stony Brook notes state the deformation retract; the item records its immediate homotopy-equivalence consequence as a separate corollary.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave6-topology.provenance.jsonl-42-{"id":"fs-a-retract-must-be-a-deformation-retract","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://pi.math.cornell.edu/~hatcher/AT/AT.pdf"],"rationale":"Hatcher explicitly says not every retraction is a deformation retraction: every space retracts onto a point, whereas deformation retraction to a point forces path-connectedness. The item chooses the two-point discrete specialization.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave6-topology.provenance.jsonl-43-{"id":"fs-homotopy-equivalent-spaces-are-homeomorphic","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://pi.math.cornell.edu/~hatcher/AT/AT.pdf"],"rationale":"Hatcher introduces homotopy equivalence as strictly broader than homeomorphism and supplies nonhomeomorphic examples. The item uses the elementary point-versus-real-line specialization.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave6-topology.provenance.jsonl:44:{"id":"ex-straight-line-homotopy-between-maps-into-rn","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://math.ucr.edu/~jbergner/SPWM09.pdf"],"rationale":"This is the convex-target straight-line theorem specialized to the full Euclidean codomain.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave6-topology.provenance.jsonl:45:{"id":"ex-intervals-and-euclidean-spaces-are-contractible","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://math.ucr.edu/~jbergner/SPWM09.pdf"],"rationale":"Intervals and Euclidean spaces are convex, so this is the source's convex-contractibility theorem specialized to those standard sets.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave6-topology.provenance.jsonl-46-{"id":"ex-a-point-and-r-are-homotopy-equivalent-not-homeomorphic","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://pi.math.cornell.edu/~hatcher/AT/AT.pdf"],"rationale":"Hatcher's contractibility and homotopy-equivalence discussion licenses the equivalence to a point; the nonhomeomorphism is the item's explicit cardinality specialization.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave6-topology.provenance.jsonl-47-{"id":"ex-a-two-point-retract-that-is-not-a-deformation-retract","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://pi.math.cornell.edu/~hatcher/AT/AT.pdf"],"rationale":"Hatcher gives exactly the general mechanism: every space retracts to a point, but a deformation retraction to a point forces path-connectedness. The two-point discrete witness is its smallest specialization.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave6-topology.provenance.jsonl-48-{"id":"ex-a-cylinder-deformation-retracts-onto-an-end","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://pi.math.cornell.edu/~hatcher/AT/AT.pdf"],"rationale":"Hatcher's deformation-retraction definition and mapping-cylinder discussion use the same product-interval contraction; this item writes the cylinder formula for an arbitrary space.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave6-topology.provenance.jsonl-49-{"id":"ex-radial-deformation-retraction-of-punctured-rn","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://www.math.stonybrook.edu/~cschnell/pdf/notes/mat530.pdf"],"rationale":"The Stony Brook notes give exactly the radial retraction of punctured Euclidean space onto the unit sphere with the same homotopy.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
research/audit/wave6-topology.provenance.jsonl:50:{"id":"ex-path-homotopy-through-straight-segments-in-a-convex-set","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://math.ucr.edu/~jbergner/SPWM09.pdf","https://pi.math.cornell.edu/~hatcher/AT/AT.pdf"],"rationale":"The straight-line homotopy into a convex set is standard in the first source, and Hatcher supplies the fixed-endpoint path-homotopy convention; the item combines them under equal endpoint hypotheses.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
